1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,835
Hello again,
hackers.
Today our target looks

2
00:00:03,835 --> 00:00:07,030
harmless:
an echo server.
We type text,

3
00:00:07,030 --> 00:00:10,120
it gives text back.
But beneath that

4
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:12,783
echo is a stack frame,
a missing

5
00:00:12,783 --> 00:00:16,617
size limit,
and one tiny return instruction

6
00:00:16,617 --> 00:00:19,600
that lets us borrow code from libc.

7
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:22,817
This is ret2libc:
not shellcode,
not guessed

8
00:00:22,817 --> 00:00:25,945
addresses,
but a traced route through code

9
00:00:25,945 --> 00:00:28,000
the process already loaded.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,033
We start with exactly what the challenge

11
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:36,734
gave us.
No friendly C source.
No

12
00:00:36,734 --> 00:00:41,174
comment that explains the bug.
Just a

13
00:00:41,174 --> 00:00:45,763
binary,
a libc,
and a Makefile fragment.

14
00:00:45,763 --> 00:00:49,760
The raw ls output stays on screen

15
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:50,500
first.

16
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:53,841
Then file tells us that vuln is

17
00:00:53,841 --> 00:00:58,918
a 64-bit dynamically linked executable and it

18
00:00:58,918 --> 00:01:02,927
is not stripped.
The supplied libc is

19
00:01:02,927 --> 00:01:07,203
a stripped 64-bit shared object.
That is

20
00:01:07,203 --> 00:01:09,875
our evidence workspace,

21
00:01:09,875 --> 00:01:14,184
and every clean teaching fragment in this

22
00:01:14,184 --> 00:01:18,000
episode will visibly descend from it.

23
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,136
The Makefile is build context,
not source.

24
00:01:23,136 --> 00:01:26,158
It says 64-bit,
no PIE,
and a

25
00:01:26,158 --> 00:01:31,143
local runpath.
The binary still gets the

26
00:01:31,143 --> 00:01:35,524
final word.
readelf says its type is

27
00:01:35,524 --> 00:01:40,962
EXEC.
GNU_STACK is readable and writable,
but

28
00:01:40,962 --> 00:01:42,926
not executable.

29
00:01:42,926 --> 00:01:47,797
The dynamic section gives us RUNPATH dot

30
00:01:47,797 --> 00:01:52,810
slash and GNU_RELRO,
while the symbol check

31
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:58,111
finds no stack canary symbol.
Together those

32
00:01:58,111 --> 00:02:01,549
rows mean:
no PIE,
NX enabled,
no

33
00:02:01,549 --> 00:02:06,276
canary,
and partial RELRO.
NX changes the

34
00:02:06,276 --> 00:02:07,852
attack shape.

35
00:02:07,852 --> 00:02:11,426
Bytes can sit on the stack,
but

36
00:02:11,426 --> 00:02:17,085
they cannot become instructions.
So our stack

37
00:02:17,085 --> 00:02:21,999
must become a route made from addresses.

38
00:02:21,999 --> 00:02:26,284
The symbol table is our map because

39
00:02:26,284 --> 00:02:30,717
this binary is not stripped.
It names

40
00:02:30,717 --> 00:02:36,775
convert_case at 0x400677,
do_stuff at 0x4006d8,
and

41
00:02:36,775 --> 00:02:41,355
main at 0x400771.
That suggests a flow

42
00:02:41,355 --> 00:02:46,526
from main into do_stuff,
then through scanf,

43
00:02:46,526 --> 00:02:49,777
a conversion loop,
and puts.

44
00:02:49,777 --> 00:02:53,871
It is only a provisional map until

45
00:02:53,871 --> 00:02:59,865
the disassembly proves it.
Notice the discipline

46
00:02:59,865 --> 00:03:04,689
here:
the friendly diagram is pulled out

47
00:03:04,689 --> 00:03:09,806
of the complete symbol table,
not invented

48
00:03:09,806 --> 00:03:11,999
on an empty canvas.

49
00:03:11,999 --> 00:03:16,476
Now we open all of do_stuff before

50
00:03:16,476 --> 00:03:22,114
isolating the bug.
The function creates a

51
00:03:22,114 --> 00:03:26,425
stack frame with sub rsp,
0x90.
It

52
00:03:26,425 --> 00:03:32,394
calculates rbp minus 0x80,
then passes that

53
00:03:32,394 --> 00:03:37,535
address to scanf.
A second scanf lands

54
00:03:37,535 --> 00:03:39,856
at rbp minus 0x81.

55
00:03:39,856 --> 00:03:46,079
The loop begins at 0x400728,
calls convert_case,

56
00:03:46,079 --> 00:03:51,345
then the function calls puts,
leaves,
and

57
00:03:51,345 --> 00:03:57,250
returns.
The format strings are not friendly

58
00:03:57,250 --> 00:04:01,558
source text;
they are raw bytes in

59
00:04:01,558 --> 00:04:05,228
rodata.
The dump says 25 5b 5e

60
00:04:05,228 --> 00:04:09,856
0a 5d 00:
percent,
open bracket,
caret,

61
00:04:09,856 --> 00:04:15,234
newline,
close bracket,
zero.
That decodes to

62
00:04:15,234 --> 00:04:21,509
percent bracket caret newline bracket:
scan until

63
00:04:21,509 --> 00:04:25,990
newline,
with no width limit.
The next

64
00:04:25,990 --> 00:04:30,323
bytes decode to percent c.
The first

65
00:04:30,323 --> 00:04:32,713
scanf is the hazard,

66
00:04:32,713 --> 00:04:37,640
and its proof is the rodata dump

67
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:41,999
joined back to the call site.

68
00:04:41,999 --> 00:04:48,007
The input begins at rbp minus 0x80.

69
00:04:48,007 --> 00:04:54,873
That places the saved base pointer 128

70
00:04:54,873 --> 00:05:01,310
bytes above the buffer,
and the saved

71
00:05:01,310 --> 00:05:08,819
return address eight bytes after that.
One

72
00:05:08,819 --> 00:05:16,758
hundred twenty-eight plus eight gives us the

73
00:05:16,758 --> 00:05:21,264
control offset:
136 bytes.

74
00:05:21,264 --> 00:05:27,626
But this challenge has a strange visible

75
00:05:27,626 --> 00:05:33,800
side effect.
do_stuff sends the first one

76
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:43,155
hundred bytes through convert_case.
The disassembly checks

77
00:05:43,155 --> 00:05:49,704
letter ranges,
then adds or subtracts 0x20.

78
00:05:49,704 --> 00:05:56,066
The loop bound compares the counter with

79
00:05:56,066 --> 00:06:03,550
0x63,
so indices zero through ninety-nine mutate.

80
00:06:03,550 --> 00:06:07,645
A wall of As can echo as

81
00:06:07,645 --> 00:06:15,381
AaAaAa.
Our return address begins at byte

82
00:06:15,381 --> 00:06:22,890
136,
safely after that mutation zone.
The

83
00:06:22,890 --> 00:06:29,715
counter itself sits at rbp minus 0x8,

84
00:06:29,715 --> 00:06:38,134
inside the overwritten region.
When the loop

85
00:06:38,134 --> 00:06:43,822
finishes,
it leaves 0x64 there:

86
00:06:43,822 --> 00:06:50,553
ASCII lowercase d.
So a trailing d

87
00:06:50,553 --> 00:06:57,035
is an echo artifact,
not the leak.

88
00:06:57,035 --> 00:07:02,769
The leak is the next thing we

89
00:07:02,769 --> 00:07:09,999
will deliberately route to stdout.

90
00:07:09,999 --> 00:07:16,355
To defeat ASLR,
we need one real

91
00:07:16,355 --> 00:07:25,254
libc address from this run.
The relocation

92
00:07:25,254 --> 00:07:32,374
table says puts uses a JUMP_SLOT at

93
00:07:32,374 --> 00:07:40,510
0x601018.
The PLT says puts at 0x400540

94
00:07:40,510 --> 00:07:46,612
jumps through that GOT entry.

95
00:07:46,612 --> 00:07:53,035
So puts at PLT can print the

96
00:07:53,035 --> 00:08:00,627
pointer stored in puts at GOT.
On

97
00:08:00,627 --> 00:08:09,094
x86-64,
the first argument is RDI.
The

98
00:08:09,094 --> 00:08:17,561
clean symbol table does not give us

99
00:08:17,561 --> 00:08:24,861
pop rdi ret,
but the byte stream

100
00:08:24,861 --> 00:08:30,992
inside libc csu init does.

101
00:08:30,992 --> 00:08:38,114
In normal decoding we see 41 5f

102
00:08:38,114 --> 00:08:44,666
c3:
pop r15,
ret.
Start one byte

103
00:08:44,666 --> 00:08:52,927
later at 0x400913,
and 5f c3 becomes

104
00:08:52,927 --> 00:09:00,904
pop rdi,
ret.
That shifted decode is

105
00:09:00,904 --> 00:09:06,031
not a guess;
it is a real

106
00:09:06,031 --> 00:09:13,153
disassembly command.
Finally,

107
00:09:13,153 --> 00:09:19,029
init gives us a plain ret at

108
00:09:19,029 --> 00:09:27,309
0x40052e.
It looks boring now.
Later it

109
00:09:27,309 --> 00:09:35,322
will be the alignment shim that lets

110
00:09:35,322 --> 00:09:42,000
the second chain survive libc.

111
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,890
Stage one now has a provenance trail

112
00:09:47,890 --> 00:09:55,154
for every block.
One hundred thirty-six bytes

113
00:09:55,154 --> 00:10:01,044
of padding come from the stack frame.

114
00:10:01,044 --> 00:10:06,934
The shifted decode gives pop rdi ret.

115
00:10:06,934 --> 00:10:13,216
The relocation table gives puts at GOT.

116
00:10:13,216 --> 00:10:17,143
The PLT gives puts at PLT.

117
00:10:17,143 --> 00:10:24,010
And the symbol table gives do_stuff again.

118
00:10:24,010 --> 00:10:28,655
The chain sets RDI to puts at

119
00:10:28,655 --> 00:10:33,907
GOT,
returns into puts at PLT,
and

120
00:10:33,907 --> 00:10:41,380
re-enters do_stuff.
On a matching runtime,
puts

121
00:10:41,380 --> 00:10:47,439
would emit ugly raw pointer bytes.
We

122
00:10:47,439 --> 00:10:52,286
do not have that runtime here.

123
00:10:52,286 --> 00:10:58,851
The actual local launch fails before the

124
00:10:58,851 --> 00:11:05,416
program begins:
the supplied libc and the

125
00:11:05,416 --> 00:11:13,719
host loader disagree at early initialization.
That

126
00:11:13,719 --> 00:11:17,388
is not a shell,
not a leak,

127
00:11:17,388 --> 00:11:21,636
and not a reason to fake one.

128
00:11:21,636 --> 00:11:27,429
It is a concrete runtime constraint,

129
00:11:27,429 --> 00:11:30,000
saved as evidence.

130
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:36,491
The supplied libc still gives us its

131
00:11:36,491 --> 00:11:45,362
internal distances.
readelf finds puts at offset

132
00:11:45,362 --> 00:11:53,367
0x80a30 and system at 0x4f4e0.
strings finds

133
00:11:53,367 --> 00:11:59,642
slash bin slash sh at 0x1b40fa.
ASLR

134
00:11:59,642 --> 00:12:05,916
can slide the entire library,
but it

135
00:12:05,916 --> 00:12:11,758
does not change those distances.

136
00:12:11,758 --> 00:12:18,363
With a real stage-one leak,
libc base

137
00:12:18,363 --> 00:12:27,019
equals leaked puts minus 0x80a30.
Then system

138
00:12:27,019 --> 00:12:33,396
is base plus 0x4f4e0,
and slash bin

139
00:12:33,396 --> 00:12:39,774
slash sh is base plus 0x1b40fa.
One

140
00:12:39,774 --> 00:12:48,201
known address would align the whole library

141
00:12:48,201 --> 00:12:49,340
ruler.

142
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:55,895
In this render those runtime values remain

143
00:12:55,895 --> 00:13:02,996
symbolic because the required matching run is

144
00:13:02,996 --> 00:13:04,999
unavailable.

145
00:13:04,999 --> 00:13:11,041
Stage two is the borrowed function call.

146
00:13:11,041 --> 00:13:18,547
Overflow again with one hundred thirty-six bytes.

147
00:13:18,547 --> 00:13:23,124
Use the ret shim at 0x40052e to

148
00:13:23,124 --> 00:13:28,616
shift the stack by one address width.

149
00:13:28,616 --> 00:13:34,078
Then pop rdi ret loads the runtime

150
00:13:34,078 --> 00:13:39,539
address of slash bin slash sh into

151
00:13:39,539 --> 00:13:45,586
RDI.
The final saved return address is

152
00:13:45,586 --> 00:13:52,218
runtime system.
In compact form,
the stack

153
00:13:52,218 --> 00:13:57,679
route becomes ret shim,
pop rdi ret,

154
00:13:57,679 --> 00:13:59,825
binsh,
system.

155
00:13:59,825 --> 00:14:05,137
The added ret is not decorative.
Some

156
00:14:05,137 --> 00:14:11,512
libc paths expect stack alignment.
The shim

157
00:14:11,512 --> 00:14:18,596
consumes eight bytes and changes the alignment

158
00:14:18,596 --> 00:14:23,731
before the route enters libc.
We can

159
00:14:23,731 --> 00:14:29,929
teach that mechanism from the real gadget

160
00:14:29,929 --> 00:14:31,877
and ABI model,

161
00:14:31,877 --> 00:14:36,128
but we do not claim a local

162
00:14:36,128 --> 00:14:41,999
shell without the matching loader.

163
00:14:41,999 --> 00:14:47,202
Here is the honesty gate.
A final

164
00:14:47,202 --> 00:14:54,407
exploit transcript must come from the real

165
00:14:54,407 --> 00:15:00,811
remote service or a local runtime with

166
00:15:00,811 --> 00:15:09,816
the matching ld-linux loader.
This workspace contains

167
00:15:09,816 --> 00:15:16,421
neither.
The captured local result is an

168
00:15:16,421 --> 00:15:26,627
ld.so inconsistency assertion during early initialization.

169
00:15:26,627 --> 00:15:32,245
That is where this proof stops.
The

170
00:15:32,245 --> 00:15:40,472
analysis does not disappear:
challenge files led

171
00:15:40,472 --> 00:15:49,501
to build context;
readelf proved protections;
symbols

172
00:15:49,501 --> 00:15:57,727
named do_stuff;
disassembly found the stack frame;

173
00:15:57,727 --> 00:16:06,355
rodata exposed the unbounded scanf;
the relocation

174
00:16:06,355 --> 00:16:10,167
table named puts at GOT;

175
00:16:10,167 --> 00:16:16,395
the PLT named puts at PLT;
shifted

176
00:16:16,395 --> 00:16:22,854
bytes revealed pop rdi ret;
and the

177
00:16:22,854 --> 00:16:32,081
provided libc supplied the offsets.
The missing

178
00:16:32,081 --> 00:16:39,693
item is only the runtime transcript,
and

179
00:16:39,693 --> 00:16:44,999
it remains visibly pending.

180
00:16:44,999 --> 00:16:53,359
The challenge-specific pitfalls are worth keeping.
First,

181
00:16:53,359 --> 00:16:59,628
AaAaAa output comes from convert_case and a

182
00:16:59,628 --> 00:17:04,330
loop bound of 0x63:
only the first

183
00:17:04,330 --> 00:17:09,555
one hundred bytes mutate,
while a ROP

184
00:17:09,555 --> 00:17:13,735
chain at 136 survives.
Second,

185
00:17:13,735 --> 00:17:20,548
the trailing d comes from the loop

186
00:17:20,548 --> 00:17:27,360
counter at rbp minus 0x8 ending at

187
00:17:27,360 --> 00:17:32,713
0x64.
It is visible,
but it is

188
00:17:32,713 --> 00:17:38,553
not the leak.
Third,
the pop rdi

189
00:17:38,553 --> 00:17:45,365
ret gadget begins in the middle of

190
00:17:45,365 --> 00:17:46,825
41 5f c3,

191
00:17:46,825 --> 00:17:51,878
so the address is 0x400913,
not the

192
00:17:51,878 --> 00:17:57,834
address of the full pop r15 instruction.

193
00:17:57,834 --> 00:18:02,346
Fourth,
a provided libc is not a

194
00:18:02,346 --> 00:18:09,564
usable local runtime unless its loader matches.

195
00:18:09,564 --> 00:18:16,422
The broader ret2libc lesson is evidence flow.

196
00:18:16,422 --> 00:18:21,656
Find the overflow.
Measure control.

197
00:18:21,656 --> 00:18:25,575
Find a way to set the first

198
00:18:25,575 --> 00:18:33,038
argument.
Leak one known libc function.
Subtract

199
00:18:33,038 --> 00:18:39,010
its offset.
Add the targets.
Then return

200
00:18:39,010 --> 00:18:45,727
into code the process already trusts.
Today,

201
00:18:45,727 --> 00:18:53,004
the stack never became executable.
The payload

202
00:18:53,004 --> 00:18:58,229
never became code.
It became a path:

203
00:18:58,229 --> 00:19:03,205
one route to leak libc,
and one

204
00:19:03,205 --> 00:19:09,632
route to call system.
That is ret2libc.

205
00:19:09,632 --> 00:19:15,438
Not a spell.
A traced route through

206
00:19:15,438 --> 00:19:19,999
the binary's own machinery.

